e waiting on them. Similarly, B5 was discarded by next player, indicating it may be safe, but not necessarily.\n- **Discard B4**: I have two B4s. It supports possible chow [B4,B5,B6] or [B3,B4,B5]. B3 is still available (4 unshown), B5 has 3 unshown, so drawing B3 or B6 could help. But keeping both B4s is speculative. With no immediate use, discarding one B4 might be acceptable.\n\nReason: Among the options, **J3** stands out as the least connected tile. I have no other knitted straight prospects (missing 1-4-7, 2-5-8, 3-6-9 structure), and I'm not building toward honors-heavy hands. My direction seems more toward suited combinations, especially in Bamboo and Characters. J3 is isolated, cannot form a chow, and there's no sign of dragon pung development. Discarding it preserves flexibility without sacrificing structural potential. In contrast, T4, T5, B5, and B4 are all part of possible All Fives (16 points) \u2014 a valuable pattern. F2 is a pair tied to Prevalent Wind, worth preserving unless forced.\n\nAnswer: Play J3"}
